Chlorofunk

Ingredients: JAZZ RAP / NATIVE TONGUE + ACID HOUSE + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Clap  
Creative Catalyst: Botanists in South American cloud forests converted plant bio-electric resistance into squelching synth triggers.

Chlorofunk is the ecstatic collision of golden-era Native Tongues Afrocentric jazz rap and 1989 Shoom-era acid house. It strips back heavy techno boom-tss to rely on infectious, hand-cranked hand claps, warm fender rhodes inversions, and squelching Roland TB-303 basslines tuned to plant-voltage micro-scales. The result is a sun-drenched, blissed-out rave music where polysyllabic positive verses meet wet, bubbling 303 filter sweeps that make warehouse floors feel like humid greenhouse danceparties.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 120–124 BPM. A swinging, un-quantized house shuffle driven by heavy natural claps and acoustic rimshots rather than dominant kick drums.
- Harmonic Palette: Major 9th jazz chords, warm Rhodes suspensions, and altered mixolydian modes derived from bio-feedback voltage mapping.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Bio-Voltage Gating: Roland TB-303 accent envelope filters triggered directly by raw plant capacitance data.
  2. Layered Handclaps: Up to eight distinct wet/dry natural handclaps stacked in a polyrhythmic call-and-response format.
  3. Cassette Tape Warmth: Master mixes ran through 1/4-inch tape at high pitch then slowed to give the acid squelch a saturated, vinyl-esque jazz sheen.
- Dynamics: Starts as an intimate jazz coffeehouse groove before a warm acid baseline blooms, erupting into a ecstatic, clapping collective euphoria.

Scene & Origin Lore

In the damp winter of 1991, two field researchers from the Kew Gardens ethnobotany department brought a modified bio-transducer and a Roland TB-303 into a squatted conservatory in South London. Feeding the electrical fluctuations of Monstera deliciosa into the 303’s CV control inputs while classic A Tribe Called Quest bootlegs played over the PA, they stumbled upon an intoxicating dynamic: the plants responded to the ambient humidity of sweat and dancing bodies by shifting the acid filter envelope. The resulting dance scene, dubbed "The Canopy," combined oversized 90s dashikis, bright yellow smiley-face badges dipped in green glitter, and sweat-soaked linen suits. Clapping circles replaced aggressive mosh pits as ravers chased the joyous, oxygen-rich trance of plant-led acid jazz.

Signature Instrumentation

- Roland TB-303 (Bio-Modded): The core acid sound, tuned to squelch dynamically based on live botanical bio-feedback voltage.
- Stacked Layered Claps: The primary percussion engine, utilizing wide-panned human handclaps, woodblocks, and 808 clap circuits.
- Fender Rhodes Mark I: Providing cozy, extended jazz-rap chords that soften the harshness of the acid synth lines.
- Acoustic Upright Bass / MPC Sampler: Sampling warm 1970s blue note upright bass solos, sliced and looped under the acid lines.

